Hard-wired HTTP calls make this class untestable; extract a transport seam. Save /workspace/geocode.py:

import json
import urllib.request

class Geocoder:
    def lookup(self, address):
        url = "https://geo.example.test/search?q=" + urllib.request.quote(address)
        with urllib.request.urlopen(url, timeout=5) as r:
            data = json.load(r)
        if not data:
            return None
        best = data[0]
        return {"lat": float(best["lat"]), "lon": float(best["lon"])}

(Note urllib.request.quote is actually urllib.parse.quote; that latent bug ships too.) Refactor: define a transport callable get_json(url) -> parsed JSON, injected into Geocoder.__init__ with a default implementation that wraps urllib properly (fixing the quote import bug in the default transport). lookup logic stays pure. Then tests/test_geocode.py with a FakeTransport recording requested URLs and returning canned payloads: address with spaces and unicode is percent-encoded in the URL; first result wins when several return; empty list gives None; strings for lat/lon are coerced to float; transport raising URLError propagates as a GeocodeError you define. No sockets in tests (autouse fixture breaking socket.socket). pytest green.

